{"task_id": "HumanEval/55", "solution": "I have generated the code for the `fib` function, along with `__init__.py` and `main.py` based on the design document. The `fib` function implements an iterative approach to calculate the n-th Fibonacci number. The `main.py` provides an entry point to run the `fib` function from the command line.```python\ndef fib(n: int):\n    \"\"\"Return n-th Fibonacci number.\n    Uses an iterative approach, assuming n is a positive integer based on 1-based indexing.\n    >>> fib(10)\n    55\n    >>> fib(1)\n    1\n    >>> fib(8)\n    21\n    \"\"\"\n    if n == 1 or n == 2:\n        return 1\n    a, b = 1, 1\n    for _ in range(n - 2):\n        a, b = b, a + b\n    return b\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/56", "solution": "```python\ndef correct_bracketing(brackets: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\" Checks if the given string of '<' and '>' characters has balanced brackets.\n    Returns True if brackets are balanced, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    balance_counter = 0\n    for char in brackets:\n        if char == '<':\n            balance_counter += 1\n        elif char == '>':\n            if balance_counter > 0:\n                balance_counter -= 1\n            else:\n                # Premature closing bracket found.\n                return False\n        # Ignore other characters, as specified in the design.\n\n    # All opening brackets must be closed for balance.\n    return balance_counter == 0\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/56", "solution": "```python\ndef correct_bracketing(brackets: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if the given string of angle brackets is correctly paired and balanced.\n\n    Args:\n        brackets: A string consisting only of '<' and '>'.\n\n    Returns:\n        True if every opening bracket '<' has a corresponding closing bracket '>',\n        and False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    bracket_count = 0\n    for char in brackets:\n        if char == '<':\n            bracket_count += 1\n        elif char == '>':\n            if bracket_count == 0:\n                return False  # Closing bracket without a matching opening bracket\n            bracket_count -= 1\n    return bracket_count == 0  # True if all opening brackets are closed\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/57", "solution": "```python\ndef _is_monotonically_increasing(l: list) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Checks if a list is monotonically increasing.\n    Returns True if each element is greater than or equal to the preceding one.\"\"\"\n    if len(l) <= 1:\n        return True\n    for i in range(1, len(l)):\n        if l[i] < l[i-1]:\n            return False\n    return True\n\ndef _is_monotonically_decreasing(l: list) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Checks if a list is monotonically decreasing.\n    Returns True if each element is less than or equal to the preceding one.\"\"\"\n    if len(l) <= 1:\n        return True\n    for i in range(1, len(l)):\n        if l[i] > l[i-1]:\n            return False\n    return True\n\ndef monotonic(l: list) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Return True is list elements are monotonically increasing or decreasing.\n    Checks if the list is either strictly increasing or strictly decreasing.\n    \"\"\"\n    return _is_monotonically_increasing(l) or _is_monotonically_decreasing(l)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/57", "solution": "def _is_monotonically_increasing(input_list: list) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Checks if a list is monotonically increasing (non-decreasing).\n    Returns True if the list is empty, has one element, or is sorted in non-decreasing order.\"\"\"\n    for i in range(len(input_list) - 1):\n        if input_list[i] > input_list[i+1]:\n            return False\n    return True\n\ndef _is_monotonically_decreasing(input_list: list) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Checks if a list is monotonically decreasing (non-increasing).\n    Returns True if the list is empty, has one element, or is sorted in non-increasing order.\"\"\"\n    for i in range(len(input_list) - 1):\n        if input_list[i] < input_list[i+1]:\n            return False\n    return True\n\ndef monotonic(l: list) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Return True if list elements are monotonically increasing or decreasing.\n    This function orchestrates internal checks for both types of monotonicity.\"\"\"\n    return _is_monotonically_increasing(l) or _is_monotonically_decreasing(l)"}
