{"task_id": "HumanEval/126", "solution": "```python\ndef is_sorted(lst):\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a list is sorted in ascending order and has no element occurring three or more times.\n    Assumes the input list contains only non-negative integers, as per the design specification.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def _is_sorted_ascending_internal(numbers):\n        \"\"\"Checks if a list of numbers is sorted in ascending order.\"\"\"\n        if not numbers:\n            return True\n        for i in range(len(numbers) - 1):\n            if numbers[i] > numbers[i + 1]:\n                return False\n        return True\n\n    def _has_excessive_duplicates_internal(numbers):\n        \"\"\"Determines if any integer in the list occurs three or more times.\"\"\"\n        counts = {}\n        for num in numbers:\n            counts[num] = counts.get(num, 0) + 1\n            if counts[num] >= 3:\n                return True\n        return False\n\n    # As per critical requirements, we trust caller's input regarding non-negative integers.\n    # The _validate_input_elements step from the design is implicitly satisfied by the problem statement\n    # \"Assume no negative numbers and only integers.\"\n\n    if not _is_sorted_ascending_internal(lst):\n        return False\n\n    if _has_excessive_duplicates_internal(lst):\n        return False\n\n    return True\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/127", "solution": "import math\n\nclass Interval:\n    \"\"\"Represents a closed integer interval.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, start: int, end: int):\n        self.start = start\n        self.end = end\n\n    def get_length(self) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Calculates the length of the interval.\"\"\"\n        return self.end - self.start + 1\n\nclass IntervalOperations:\n    \"\"\"Handles operations related to interval intersection and primality checks.\"\"\"\n    def _is_prime(self, n: int) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Checks if an integer n is a prime number.\"\"\"\n        if n <= 1:\n            return False\n        if n == 2:\n            return True\n        if n % 2 == 0:\n            return False\n        for i in range(3, int(math.sqrt(n)) + 1, 2):\n            if n % i == 0:\n                return False\n        return True\n\n    def _calculate_intersection_interval(self, interval1: Interval, interval2: Interval) -> Interval | None:\n        \"\"\"Calculates the intersection of two Interval objects.\"\"\"\n        intersection_start = max(interval1.start, interval2.start)\n        intersection_end = min(interval1.end, interval2.end)\n\n        if intersection_start > intersection_end:\n            return None\n        return Interval(intersection_start, intersection_end)\n\n    def intersection(self, interval1_tuple: tuple[int, int], interval2_tuple: tuple[int, int]) -> str:\n        \"\"\"Determines if the length of the intersection of two intervals is prime.\"\"\"\n        interval_obj1 = Interval(interval1_tuple[0], interval1_tuple[1])\n        interval_obj2 = Interval(interval2_tuple[0], interval2_tuple[1])\n\n        intersection_interval = self._calculate_intersection_interval(interval_obj1, interval_obj2)\n\n        if intersection_interval is None:\n            return \"NO\"\n\n        intersection_length = intersection_interval.get_length()\n        if self._is_prime(intersection_length):\n            return \"YES\"\n        else:\n            return \"NO\"\n\ndef intersection(interval1, interval2):\n    \"\"\"\n    Determines if the length of the intersection of two intervals is a prime number.\n    Returns \"YES\" if prime, \"NO\" otherwise or if intervals don't intersect.\n    \"\"\"\n    operations = IntervalOperations()\n    return operations.intersection(interval1, interval2)"}
